WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.660
Speaker 1: We have a voicemail number. That number&nbsp;
is 2192. David P Here is a call about tipping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:12.660
a subject about which I feel very strongly.
Speaker 4: Do you like to give tips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:15.120
at restaurants? No.
Speaker 1: But I do it.&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.440
Speaker 4: I think that people&nbsp;
should stop tipping at restaurants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:28.560
You might say, well, that's bad for the waitress.&nbsp;
Well, if you keep tipping at a restaurant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:36.180
The restaurant owner. We'll&nbsp;
continue to not pay a living wage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:41.340
To the waitress of our country.&nbsp;
If everyone stops tipping.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:47.340
Then obviously the waitress and bartender&nbsp;
will leave and not work at the restaurant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:55.020
And now the restaurant is stuck with food that&nbsp;
they can serve to their customers. Mm hmm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:58.080
And when that happens, they will either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:08.040
close shop. Or reevaluate their payroll.
Speaker 1: And they will also reevaluate their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:13.500
prices. So, listen, I want to I've been upfront.&nbsp;
I hate tipping culture and how it has developed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:21.900
I don't like the inconsistency of it. I don't like&nbsp;
the increased nature of more and more places where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:27.840
you're you're allowed to tip. A lot of this is&nbsp;
driven by companies like Square and Stripe who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:34.140
put those payment tablets. They collect a portion&nbsp;
of every dollar. So it's to the advantage of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:40.500
companies that do the payment tablets to offer&nbsp;
tipping for everything. And then, hey, we'll just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:49.500
collect more money. I tip well everywhere I go,&nbsp;
I tip housekeeping when I stay in a hotel and I,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:56.760
you know, the valet people, I, I tip everywhere.&nbsp;
But I am very, very much against the entire thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:01.800
The problem with saying if we just stop tipping,&nbsp;
the restaurants will have to adjust. First of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:06.060
the tipping has gone way beyond restaurants at&nbsp;
this point. And the first thing that's going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:11.700
to happen is if restaurants immediately&nbsp;
play that out. And by the way, some states,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:21.000
some state law allow the my understanding is that&nbsp;
if the tips don't get that tipped wage up to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:26.160
minimum wage, the restaurant owner already has to&nbsp;
make up that difference. That's my belief. But we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:31.500
should we should fact check that in the immediate&nbsp;
if restaurants all of a sudden because everybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:38.520
stop tipping needed to pay employees dramatically&nbsp;
more, they would immediately increase prices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:45.480
significantly at the restaurant. Now, this is&nbsp;
effectively what has happened. The tips subsidize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:50.820
and this is what has happened at restaurants that&nbsp;
now say we include a service charge. Please don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:59.340
tip. And the prices already reflect that the $12&nbsp;
sandwich might just cost you 15, but they will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:03.960
say, please don't tip. We have a service charge.&nbsp;
Many restaurants in New York City have already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:08.040
done this. You look at the menu and it says we&nbsp;
are we pay our employees. Well. The prices already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:15.180
reflect that or we have added a 4% surcharge for&nbsp;
the back of the house and tip is already inclusive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:20.760
on the prices or whatever the case may be.&nbsp;
Honestly, I'm I'm more or less for that sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:25.860
thing. And I know that at least in the immediate,&nbsp;
we're just going to pay more in order to not tip.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:31.680
But there are a bunch of different issues with the&nbsp;
tipping model, and it includes that when people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:36.780
come from different countries, you don't always&nbsp;
know what's expected. I was recently in the UK.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:43.860
The UK has now taken to they automatically include&nbsp;
12 and a half percent. So you don't have to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:48.060
about it. You can ask for it to be removed,&nbsp;
but the bill comes out and it automatically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:54.300
says 12 and a half percent tip. And what I like&nbsp;
about that is I don't have to think about it. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.300 --> 00:04:01.560
just is what it is. But my preference would&nbsp;
be eliminate tipping, put real pricing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:06.780
reflects the total cost of paying employees&nbsp;
well on the menu. And then I can look at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:10.500
menu and determine whether I think that this is a&nbsp;
restaurant that I want to visit. But I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:15.240
if we all just stop tipping cold turkey, it'll be&nbsp;
terrible for workers. I don't believe it will have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:21.000
the effect that our caller believes it will have.&nbsp;
